 ],
 "literal": "On the gravel-having slope, raising a cairn, / with a red-flower wreath woven by splitting bowstring hemp, / adorning with beautiful peacock feathers, engraving his name, / now they indeed planted the stone. With calves / milch cows having brought back, enemies who drove away, / that the great hero passed away not knowing, / even today will they come, the bards' family?",
 "translation": "On the gravelly slope, they have raised a cairn and planted a hero stone, engraving his name, adorning it with beautiful peacock feathers and a wreath of red flowers woven with split bowstring hemp. Unaware that the great hero—who rescued the milch cows along with their calves and drove away the enemies—has passed away, will the family of bards still come here today seeking him?",
 "modern": "பருக்கைக் கற்கள் நிறைந்த பக்கத்திலே கல் குவியலைச் சேர்த்து, மரல் நாரைப் பிளந்து தொடுத்த செந்நிறப் பூமாலை சூட்டி, அழகான மயில்தோகையை அணிவித்து, அவனது பெயரைப் பொறித்து, இப்போது நடுகல்லை நட்டுவிட்டார்கள். கன்றுகளோடு பசுக்களை மீட்டுக்கொண்டு வந்து பகைவர்களை விரட்டியடித்த அந்தப் பெருந்தகையாளன் இறந்துவிட்டதை அறியாமல், இன்றும் பாணர்களுடைய சுற்றம் அவனிடம் பரிசு பெற வருவார்களோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Karantai tiṇai (recovering cattle) and the theme of Kaiyaṟunilai (lamentation). The poet expresses deep grief over the death of a hero who died defending his community's cattle. The villagers have erected a hero stone (naṭukal) on a gravelly slope, decorated with peacock feathers and red flowers, and inscribed his name upon it. The poet laments that the traveling bards, who rely on the hero's generosity, are unaware of his death and might still arrive today expecting his patronage. The imagery of the decorated hero stone on the barren slope highlights the tragic transition from a living, generous protector to a silent monument.",
